--TEST--
`executeMany` takes an array of sets and not `execute`'s flat `$params`, so a batch of one statement cannot be written as a batch of one run's values — `rule:core-classes/db-parameters` and `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::executeMany
// `rule:core-classes/db-parameters` binds values beside the statement, and the batch says how many
// times to do it: the second argument holds one inner array per run, each of
// them exactly the `$params` `Core\Db\Connection::execute` takes. The two
// shapes are one level of nesting apart, and the flat one is what a caller
// writes when they mean `execute` — so it is refused here rather than read as
// a single run whose values are the sets.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// The accepted shape: two runs of one value each.
array<array<mixed>> $sets = [];
$sets[] = ["ada"];
$sets[] = ["bo"];
uint $written = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (owner) values (?)", $sets);

// And the refused one, which is the same two values written the way `execute`
// takes them.
array<mixed> $flat = ["ada", "bo"];
uint $wrong = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (owner) values (?)", $flat);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `array<array<mixed>>`, found `array<mixed>`
   --> case.nvs:20:75
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
